"# Snapshot Isolation Analysis: T1, T2, T3 on {A, B}\n\n## 1. Assumptions\n\nThe history lists each transaction's operation sequence; the three transactions run **concurrently** (per-transaction order preserved, global interleaving unspecified). Under SI with versioning, every read returns the newest version **committed before the transaction's snapshot**, and writers are validated at commit (**first-committer-wins**). I analyze both the case where the writers overlap and the case they don't.\n\n## 2. Isolation Level Determination\n\n| Observation | Implication |\n|---|---|\n| T3 (read-only) reads A and B from a single consistent snapshot | No fractured/partial read — consistent with SI |\n| T1 and T2 read-then-write the **same items** (A, B) | Write–write conflict; SI resolves via first-committer-wins |\n| No dirty reads possible (reads see only committed versions) | Consistent with SI / versioned MVCC |\n\n**Conclusion:** The workload semantics are those of **Snapshot Isolation (SI)**. Notably, the history as written (T1, T2, T3 all commit) is *only valid under SI if T1 and T2's write sets did not overlap in time* — i.e., one writer's snapshot must include the other's commit. Otherwise SI must abort one of them.\n\n## 3. Conflict Matrix\n\n| Pair | On A | On B | Type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 ↔ T2 | R/W, W/R, W/W | R/W, W/R, W/W | Full read–write–write overlap → **lost-update shape** (not write skew) |\n| T1 → T3 | W(A) vs R(A) | W(B) vs R(B) | Reads-from, determined by T3's snapshot |\n| T2 → T3 | W(A) vs R(A) | W(B) vs R(B) | Reads-from, determined by T3's snapshot |\n\n## 4. Serialization Graph\n\n**Case A — T1 and T2 overlap and both commit (naive/weak SI, no WW validation).**\nVersion order: A: A₀ < A₁(T1) < A₂(T2); B: B₀ < B₁(T1) < B₂(T2); assume T3 snapshots after both commits.\n\n```\nT0 ──rf──> T1        T0 ──rf──> T2        (both read initial versions A0, B0)\nT2 ──rw(A)──> T1     T2 ──rw(B)──> T1    (T2 reads A0/B0; next version is T1's)\nT1 ──wo(A)──> T2     T1 ──wo(B)──> T2    (version order A1<A2, B1<B2)\nT1 ──rf──> T3        T2 ──rf──> T3\n```\n\nCycle: **T1 → T2 → T1** (write-order edge + rw-antidependency on A, again on B). This is a **G-single cycle** → **not serializable**.\n\n**Case B — Proper SI enforced (first-committer-wins).**\nT1 commits first; T2's validation detects that A (and B) advanced past T2's snapshot → **T2 aborts with a serialization failure**. Committed projection:\n\n```\nT1 ──rf──> T3        (T3 snapshots after T1, reads A1, B1)\n```\n\nAcyclic → **serializable**. Equivalent serial order: **T1 → T3** (or T3 → T1 if T3's snapshot precedes T1's commit; symmetric result **T2 → T3** if T2 commits first).\n\n## 5. Anomalies\n\n| Anomaly | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Lost update** (T1, T2 both RMW A and B) | **Latent** — would occur in Case A; **prevented** by first-committer-wins (one writer aborts) |\n| Dirty read | Impossible — versioned snapshot reads |\n| Non-repeatable read | Impossible — fixed snapshot per transaction |\n| Phantom | Not applicable (no range predicates) |\n| Write skew | Not present — both writers read *and* write both items (lost-update shape, not skew) |\n| Fractured read (T3) | Impossible — T3 reads one consistent snapshot |\n\n## 6. Result\n\n- **Isolation level:** Snapshot Isolation with MVCC.\n- **Serialization graph:** Acyclic in the SI-committed projection; contains a **cycle T1 ⇄ T2 (G-single)** if the engine fails to enforce first-committer-wins.\n- **Verdict:** **Serializable** — the committed history is equivalent to the serial order **{first-committer} → T3**, at the cost of aborting one writer (T1 or T2) with a serialization failure.\n- **Caveat:** If this engine were configured as *weak* SI (last-writer-wins, no WW check), the graph would be non-serializable and T1's updates to A and B would be **silently lost**. Under plain SERIALIZABLE (SSI/2PL), the same abort decision would be made, so the workload's outcome is identical at the serializable level."